  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 944 PM MST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nMILD AND DRY CONDITIONS THROUGH THANKSGIVING:\n\nA ridge is situated overhead with little more than high clouds\npassing through the region. Thanksgiving Day will be \ncharacterized by near-normal moisture, and as a result, region- \nwide weather will be calm. Temperatures will be 5 to 10 degrees \nabove normal Thanksgiving Day as well.\n\nSNOWY AFTER THANKSGIVING INTO THE WEEKEND:\n\nOn Friday, moisture originating from a PacNW AR will descend \ninto the Intermountain West through northwesterly flow. The bulk\nof that moisture will reside to our north, but ensembles are \nindicating that the northeastern corner of our CWA will get \nclipped by PWAT's up to 200% of normal. This sparks our next \nround of impactful weather. The onset of measurable \nprecipitation is expected to occur late afternoon to early \nevening Friday with most precipitation driven by orographics and\nanchored to the high terrain. Though moisture is expected to \nbegin declining around sunrise Saturday, models are showing the \nstrongest snow rates during this period. This is mostly driven \nby a transient shortwave embedded in the northwesterly flow, and\nas such, we should see an uptick in snow rates both over the \nmountains and into some higher elevation valleys and basins in \nnorth-central Colorado through the late morning and into the \nafternoon.\n\nAs seen in the latest forecast, snow totals are on the rise. \nWith the upward trend of snow totals for the weekend storm, the \nlow end snow scenario looks safely at or above advisory level \n(6+ inches) for the Park Range. The NBM backs this up with a\ngreater than 80% probability of 6 or more inches of snow above \n9000 feet, which would include Rabbit Ears Pass. Below this \nelevation though, probability of advisory-level snow \naccumulations sharply drop off. For those areas in the Park \nRange expected to receive the bulk of snowfall, the question is \nwhat the high end scenario could be. Per the NBM, probabilities \nof accumulations exceeding a foot stand at over 70%. In spite of\nthe high chances, we are cautious to call for 12+ inches over \nthe Park Range just yet due to lacking consistency between \nforecasts. Though guidance is trending towards higher \naccumulations, we will wait to see where model guidance settles \nbefore speculating on amounts.\n\nThough impacts look greatest for the Park Range, concerns for \nimpactful snowfall over the Flat Tops and central Colorado \nmountains are manifesting as well. The former is looking more \nand more favorable to hit advisory-level snow. Confidence has \ndecreased on exact values over the past twelve hours, but this \nis due to 75th percentile snow forecasts jumping up. So, just \nlike the Park Range, forecasts are trending upwards for snow \ntotals in the Flat Tops. As for the latter, confidence in \naccumulations has notably increased according to the NBM, though\nit is still anticipated to see the least precipitation among \nthe areas of interest. Given the northerly track of the storm, \nelevations at pass level and below are likely to struggle to \nreach 6 inches. However, with roads likely to be busier with \npost-holiday travel, even lighter accumulations will pose \nheightened travel risks. As such, be mindful of difficult \ndriving conditions through the mountains should you find \nyourself on the roads Saturday.",
